, inclusive?\", \"Look at the {v} {year} calendar. How many weeks are there from {v} {start_date} to {v} {end_date}, inclusive?\", \"{end} - {start} + 1 = {days} {count_noun}.\", \"Look at the {month_name} {year} {cal_word}. How many {unit_word} are there from {month_name} {day1} to {month_name} {day2}, inclusive?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"F06 computes days as end minus start plus 1, but the declared domains let end (3 to 31) fall below start (1 to 24), and the reason_kinds rule says the arithmetic range is the image of the two domains, so days can be zero or negative; the template then prints a false equation. F02 states that days make weeks in the form N days are A weeks, but days is computed from the dates and is not tied to answer by 7 times answer; any days value that is not a multiple of 7, or a day count of 1 paired with the plural day noun (count_noun is chosen independently of days), prints a false or ungrammatical hint. F03, F04 and F08 print a start date and an end date from independent domains with no stated ordering, so a problem can ask for the span from a later date to an earlier date in one month, which has no valid answer and gives a misleading hint. F05 and F08 ask for a count of weeks over spans the packet does not constrain to whole weeks, and the packet does not show how the key treats a remainder of days. Words: 'inclusive' appears in F04, F05 and F08 and is not in cumulative_vocab or NOT_YET_KNOWN; F03 scaffolds it, so it is noted, not counted as a failure. Clauses are producible from F04 and F05; F01, F07 and F09 are not at fault.\"}",
